<v Chaya Lipschutz>And left it there. And then I went back in the house. So this way, you know, I just-- I didn't leave with that much, maybe a shopping bag, to go-- oh, gosh.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>留在那里。然后我回到屋里。所以这样，你知道，我——我离开时没带多少东西，也许就是一个购物袋，去——噢，天哪。

00:47:52.580 --> 00:48:05.330
<v Chaya Lipschutz>But you know what? So that was hard for me, because I don't like to lie. But that-- it was all to do a good thing. It wasn't anything selfish.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>但你知道吗？所以那对我来说很艰难，因为我不喜欢说谎。但那——那一切都是为了做一件好事。那不是自私的。

00:48:05.330 --> 00:48:32.170
<v Chaya Lipschutz>You know what I mean? It was all-- you know, sometimes you're-- listen, I don't want people to think you're allowed to do white lies, but sometimes you have to. Sometimes you have to. You have no choice. I'm doing this to save another person's life, so I'm sure, as a result of what I did, God's going to forgive me for all those white lies.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>你懂我的意思吗？那一切都是——你知道，有时你是——听着，我不希望人们认为你可以撒小谎，但有时你必须这么做。有时你必须这样做。你没有选择。我这样做是为了挽救另一个人的生命，所以我确信，作为我所做之事的后果，上帝会原谅我所有那些小谎言。

00:48:32.170 --> 00:48:57.910
<v Sarah Koenig>It's one thing to plan to donate your kidney and not tell your mother. It's another thing to actually have your organ removed and not tell your mother. So Chaya had to figure out some way to break it to her once it was a done deal. And her scheme for doing this is so complicated, it makes all the earlier lies look really junior varsity. What happened is that, by chance, the same week of her surgery, somebody told Chaya about this 23-year-old Hasidic woman who had also donated a kidney to a stranger.
<v Sarah Koenig>计划捐肾不告诉母亲是一回事。真正把器官切除却不告诉母亲又是另一回事。所以查雅必须想办法在事情已经搞定后向她坦白。她策划这件事的过程如此复杂，让之前所有的谎言看起来都像业余爱好。发生的情况是，机缘巧合，在她手术的同一周，有人告诉查雅有一个23岁的哈西德女性也给陌生人捐了肾。

00:48:57.910 --> 00:49:27.430
<v Chaya Lipschutz>And I met this very lovely young woman, Feigi. I said to her, would you be willing to tell my mother-- to come over to my mother's house after the surgery and tell her that she donated a kidney and then tell her, by the way, I donated a kidney, and I'm in the hospital right now? Like, let her be the one to tell her because my mother will see that she's healthy, she looks healthy, and she's young, and she doesn't look like she had major surgery a few months earlier.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>我遇到了这位非常可爱的年轻女性，菲吉。我对她说，你愿意告诉我的母亲吗——在手术后去我母亲家，告诉她你捐了一个肾，然后告诉她，顺便说一下，我也捐了一个肾，我现在就在医院里？就像，让她成为告诉母亲那个人，因为我母亲会看到她很健康，她看起来很健康，而且她很年轻，看起来不像几个月前刚做过大手术的样子。

00:49:27.430 --> 00:49:48.680
<v Chaya Lipschutz>And so I told her, call my mother and tell her this way that you have tzedakah-- charity-- to give to one of my mother's charities. And that was a good way to get into the house. And I arranged it that my kidney recipient's family is going to call her after the surgery.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>所以我告诉她，打电话给我母亲，用这种方式告诉她，你有“tzedakah”——慈善——要捐给我母亲的一个慈善机构。那是进屋的一个好办法。我安排好我肾脏接受者的家人在手术后给她打电话。

00:49:48.680 --> 00:50:00.670
<v Chaya Lipschutz>So she called my mother. And my mother was almost out the door. And she says, no, please. Please wait. I have tzedakah to give-- the charity to give to one of your charities. And of course, my mother waited.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>于是她给我母亲打了电话。我母亲快要出门了。她说，不，请等一下。请等一下。我有tzedakah要捐——捐给你的一个慈善机构。当然，我母亲等待了。

00:50:00.670 --> 00:50:20.350
<v Chaya Lipschutz>And so she sat down with her, and she said she donated a kidney. And my mother looked at her up and down and says, she's normal and healthy and she just did something like that three months earlier. And she came with a cute little baby. And then she said to her, by the way, your daughter's right now in the hospital, and she did the same thing.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>于是她坐下来，说她捐了一个肾。我母亲上下打量她说，她看起来正常又健康，而且三个月前刚做了那种事。她还带着一个可爱的婴儿。然后她对母亲说，顺便说一下，你女儿现在就在医院里，她也做了同样的事情。

00:50:20.350 --> 00:50:23.500
<v Sarah Koenig>To everyone's relief, Chaya's mother did not have a heart attack.
<v Sarah Koenig>让所有人欣慰的是，查雅的母亲没有心脏病发作。

00:50:23.500 --> 00:50:30.790
<v Chaya Lipschutz>My mother smiled, and she said, it's mena shamayim. It's from heaven. It's heaven. You know, it's a heavenly thing that was meant to be.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>我母亲笑了，她说，这是“mena shamayim”。这是来自天堂的。这是天堂。你知道，这是命中注定的天堂之事。

00:50:30.790 --> 00:50:51.850
<v Chaya Lipschutz>And she took it very, very well, just like I thought. It was like it was just exactly according to my script, the way everything worked out. And she was happy. She was happy. And you know I was so happy it was all over, and that she took it well. And she was proud of me. And it was such a relief.
<v Chaya Lipschutz>她接受得非常、非常从容，就像我想的那样。就像一切都完全按照我的剧本进行一样。她很高兴。她很高兴。而且你知道我很高兴这一切都结束了，她接受得很好。她为我感到骄傲。那真是一种解脱。

00:50:51.850 --> 00:50:55.440
<v Sarah Koenig>It sounds like dealing with your mother was so much harder than actually donating a kidney.
